The training observations show a clear hierarchy in 0.2 % proof strength: solution_aged specimens are systematically stronger than quench_temper, quenched or annealed material, and the wrought route is stronger than AM or cast. All of the highest recorded values (712, 698, 694, 677, 674, 668 MPa \ldots) therefore belong to the solution_aged + wrought subset and cluster at aging temperatures of roughly 560–710 °C with moderate carbon (0.1–0.6 wt %).

Among the 100 untested recipes the same process window is occupied by only nine candidates. Direct compositional and processing analogues of the strongest training points further reduce the list:

- c008 lies close to both the 648 MPa and 697 MPa training examples (Ni–Mo–Co–C, aging ~680 °C);
- c038 matches the 613 MPa example (low-Mo, ~610 °C aging);
- c021 is nearest the 668 MPa low-C example;
- c015 and c052 sit inside the same aging-temperature band and possess precipitation-forming additions (Al, Ti, V) already shown to be effective.

The remaining solution_aged + wrought recipes have either far-off aging temperatures, extreme cooling rates, or missing key solid-solution/precipitate formers and are therefore ranked lower. Consequently the five candidates predicted to possess the highest yield strengths are, in descending order:

Final: c008, c038, c021, c015, c052
